    sections: [
      { heading: "Low-pressure supply side", paragraphs: ["Fuel leaves the tank pickup and travels forward through the chassis line to the engine-compartment filter head. The assembly filters fuel, separates water, provides a hand primer, and includes a bleed point. From the clean outlet, fuel reaches the high-pressure pump inlet.", "Because this section is normally under suction on a stock light-duty LB7, an air leak may cause hard starting or loss of prime without leaving a puddle. A vacuum-side restriction can also starve the pump while the filter appears externally dry."] },
      { heading: "High-pressure common-rail side", paragraphs: ["The pump raises fuel to commanded rail pressure and feeds the two cylinder-bank rails. The rails act as accumulators and distribute fuel to eight electronically controlled injectors. Pressure control depends on pump metering, rail pressure feedback, injector operation, cranking speed, and adequate low-side filling.", "Common-rail fuel can penetrate skin. Do not crack a line to check for fuel, place a hand near a suspected leak, or reuse a damaged sealing surface. Follow GM's depressurization, cleanliness, line-replacement, and torque requirements."] },
      { heading: "Return and leak-off path", paragraphs: ["Internal leakage needed for component lubrication and control exits through injector and pump return paths and goes back toward the tank. Excessive return can prevent commanded pressure, but the location cannot be identified by total return alone.", "A proper test isolates the component groups using specified containers and adapters. Results are sensitive to fuel temperature, battery/cranking condition, test duration, and whether the engine is cranking or running."] },
      { heading: "Stock versus lift-pump conversion", bullets: ["An added electric lift pump changes the low-side pressure state and priming behavior.", "Added filters, check valves, sumps, draw straws, and regulators must appear on the as-built diagram.", "Excessive lift-pump pressure can create new filter-head or return issues.", "Record brand, part numbers, pressure setting, and hose direction before troubleshooting."] },
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: "Does a stock LB7 have an electric lift pump?", a: "The common 2001–2004 light-duty Silverado/Sierra LB7 system is suction-fed by the engine-mounted high-pressure pump. Verify medium-duty or modified applications separately." },
      { q: "Why can an LB7 lose prime without leaking fuel outside?", a: "The tank-to-pump path is under suction in the stock light-duty layout, so a poor seal can draw air inward rather than push diesel outward." },
      { q: "What does the filter-head hand primer do?", a: "It fills the low-pressure supply path and helps remove air after filter service. It does not pressurize the common rail." },
      { q: "Can I bleed the LB7 by loosening injector lines?", a: "No. Use the filter-head primer and GM procedure. Opening common-rail lines while cranking or running is dangerous." },
      { q: "Does this route apply after adding a lift pump?", a: "The engine-side rail and return concept remains, but the low-pressure routing and pressures change. Use the conversion maker's diagram and document every added component." },
    ],
    sources: [gmLb7Electrical, densoCommonRail],
    reviewed,
