    title: "Toyota 22R Vacuum Hose Diagram: How to Match the Correct Routing",
    metaDescription: "Toyota 22R vacuum-hose routing guide explaining federal vs. California emissions, carburetor port types, EGR, TVSV/BVSV, distributor advance, charcoal canister, air cleaner and brake-booster circuits.",
    answer: "There is no single correct vacuum-hose schematic for every carbureted Toyota 22R. Routing changes by model year, federal/California or Canadian emissions, manual/automatic transmission, altitude package and carburetor calibration. Use the emissions/vacuum label under the hood or the matching Toyota repair-manual diagram, then identify each carburetor port and vacuum-switching valve before connecting hoses.",
    detail: "A functional map can explain what each branch does, but it cannot safely assign every nipple without the vehicle's calibration. Distributor advance, EGR modulation, charcoal-canister purge, hot-air cleaner controls and dashpots may use different combinations of ported vacuum, manifold vacuum, delay valves and temperature switches.",
    scope: "North American carbureted 22R pickup and 4Runner-era systems. EFI 22R-E, turbo 22R-TE, non-North-American calibrations and emissions-deleted engines require separate diagrams.",
    values: [
      { label: "First identifier", value: "Underhood vacuum/emissions label", note: "Match model year and emissions family" },
      { label: "Factory manual", value: "Toyota repair information", note: "Select exact year, engine, transmission and market" },
      { label: "Vacuum sources", value: "Manifold and ported", note: "Not interchangeable" },
      { label: "Temperature control", value: "TVSV/BVSV-style switching", note: "Port count and logic vary" },
      { label: "Safety-critical branch", value: "Brake booster + check valve", note: "Keep isolated from small control tees" },
    ],
    diagram: { type: "oil", title: "22R vacuum-system identification map", caption: "This map groups the branches. Use the label for exact nipple-to-nipple routing, delay-valve color and switch-port assignment.", points: ["Intake manifold vacuum", "Carburetor ported vacuum", "Brake booster/check valve", "PCV system", "Distributor advance", "EGR modulator/valve", "TVSV/BVSV switches", "Charcoal canister/purge", "Air-cleaner hot-air control", "Dashpot/idle controls"] },
    intro: [
      "The dense hose bundle on a carbureted 22R is a calibrated control network. Some hoses see vacuum at idle, some only as the throttle opens, and some are opened or blocked according to coolant temperature. Reconnecting all nipples to any available tee can make the engine run while disabling spark advance, EGR, purge or cold-driveability controls.",
      "Toyota placed a routing label in the engine compartment because the calibration belongs to the vehicle, not merely the 22R casting. If that label is missing, use the year, VIN/build information, emissions family, transmission and carburetor identification to obtain the matching Toyota diagram. Photograph valve colors, stamped port letters and one-way-valve arrows before disturbing anything.",
    ],
    steps: [
      "Record model year, pickup/4Runner chassis, transmission, market, emissions family and high-altitude equipment from the underhood labels.",
      "Verify the engine is a carbureted 22R, not a 22R-E/22R-TE, and record the carburetor identification and any replacement unit.",
      "Find the Toyota vacuum routing label or exact repair-manual schematic; do not merge diagrams from two years.",
      "Tag every hose at both ends. Record colored stripes, restrictors, delay valves, check-valve arrows, tees and thermal-switch port letters.",
      "Replace hose one circuit at a time with vacuum-rated hose of the same inside diameter and heat/fuel resistance.",
      "After routing, test base timing with the specified hoses connected/disconnected, EGR function, purge, choke/dashpot behavior, brake assist and vacuum leaks according to the exact manual.",
    ],
    sections: [
      { heading: "Manifold vacuum versus ported vacuum", paragraphs: ["Manifold vacuum is strong below the closed throttle plate at warm idle and falls as the throttle opens. Ported vacuum is taken near the throttle edge and is low or absent at the specified idle position, then rises as the throttle opens. Distributor and EGR control circuits may combine these signals through switches and modulators.", "Test a questionable source with a gauge and the exact warm-idle conditions. Nipple position alone is not proof after a carburetor has been replaced or modified."] },
      { heading: "Distributor advance branch", paragraphs: ["The distributor vacuum diaphragm changes ignition timing according to the calibrated source. Some 22R systems route the source through thermal or switching valves. Connecting directly to manifold vacuum may produce a large idle-timing change; connecting to the wrong port may remove cruise advance.", "Set base timing only with the specified connector or hose state and idle speed. Then reconnect the system and confirm that advance changes smoothly without a leaking diaphragm."] },
      { heading: "EGR and temperature-switching branch", paragraphs: ["The EGR valve is not normally given raw manifold vacuum at all times. A modulator and ported signal meter operation, while coolant-temperature switching can inhibit EGR during warm-up. California and federal diagrams can assign different ports and valves.", "A crossed TVSV/BVSV hose may look harmless but reverse when a circuit is enabled. Preserve port letters and the factory schematic rather than using switch color alone."] },
      { heading: "Canister, PCV and air-cleaner branches", paragraphs: ["The charcoal canister stores fuel vapor and purges it under calibrated conditions. The PCV valve meters crankcase vapor continuously under manifold vacuum, with filtered makeup air entering elsewhere. These systems are related to intake flow but should not be combined into an improvised tee.", "The thermostatic air-cleaner motor uses vacuum to select heated or cool intake air during warm-up. Its small hoses and thermal sensor belong to the air-cleaner control branch, not the distributor or EGR branch."] },
      { heading: "Hose-restoration checklist", bullets: ["Use vacuum-rated hose; fuel vapor and heat resistance matter near carburetor and EGR parts.", "Match inside diameter so tees and calibrated restrictors seal correctly.", "Retain delay valves, check valves and orifices in the original direction.", "Route away from throttle linkage, exhaust heat and sharp brackets.", "Never tee control hoses into the brake-booster line.", "Keep removed emissions parts for identification even if the engine arrived partially disconnected."] },
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: "Why are there different 22R vacuum diagrams online?", a: "Toyota changed routing by year, market, emissions package, transmission and carburetor calibration. Several diagrams can be genuine but apply to different trucks." },
      { q: "Can I use a 22R-E vacuum diagram on a carbureted 22R?", a: "No. EFI and carbureted induction/control systems have different components and port logic." },
      { q: "Where is the correct routing diagram on the truck?", a: "Look for the vacuum/emissions information label under the hood or in the engine compartment. If missing, use the exact Toyota repair information selected by vehicle configuration." },
      { q: "Are the two ports on a thermal vacuum switch interchangeable?", a: "Do not assume so. Some switches use multiple ports and calibrated internal states; follow the labeled port assignment." },
      { q: "Can I delete the vacuum hoses and cap everything?", a: "That can disable spark, EGR, purge, choke, dashpot or air-cleaner functions and may violate emissions law. Diagnose and restore the application-specific system." },
    ],
    sources: [toyotaTis, toyotaVacuumLabel],
