    diagram: { type: "oil", title: "Interactive Ford 3G conversion map", caption: "Select original 1G, 2G or bare-harness architecture first. The map separates low-current regulator functions from the protected B+ output path.", points: ["Battery positive", "Battery-side starter relay junction", "Mega fuse/fusible protection", "3G B+ stud", "Regulator A sense", "Ignition switch RUN", "Charge lamp/resistance", "Regulator I", "Regulator S", "Stator tap", "Alternator case ground", "Engine/battery ground"] },
    intro: [
      "A 3G alternator is popular because it produces more current at useful engine speeds and uses an internal regulator, but the conversion is not simply a plug swap. The original charge cable, ammeter loop and 2G connector may be unable to carry the new output safely. The high-current path must be redesigned at the same time as the regulator wiring.",
      "Powermaster and PA Performance publish separate conversion instructions for external-regulator 1G, internal-regulator 2G and bare-harness vehicles. Their diagrams agree on the functional core: B+ carries output, A senses system voltage, S receives the stator signal and I is the switched indicator/excitation circuit. This page preserves those distinctions instead of assigning one color chart to every classic Ford.",
    ],
    steps: [
      "Identify the original system as 1G external regulator, 2G internal regulator, 1986/two-wire variation or custom/no harness; record 3G part/output rating and mounting clock.",
      "Disconnect battery negative and remove the old alternator only after labeling BAT/output, field, stator, indicator and ground conductors.",
      "Install the correct conversion regulator plug: A to its approved B+ sense point, S to the 3G stator tap and I to the verified key-on warning/excitation circuit.",
      "Run a new high-current B+ cable from the 3G output stud to the battery-positive or battery-side starter-relay junction through protection sized/located by the alternator and kit manufacturer.",
      "Do not use the 2G push-in output connector. Isolate unused original wires individually; some can remain energized.",
      "Add an alternator-case-to-engine ground lead and confirm battery-to-engine/body grounds, bracket metal contact, pulley alignment and belt wrap/tension.",
      "Reconnect the battery, verify key-on warning behavior, start the engine and measure output, cable drops and temperature under full electrical load.",
    ],
    sections: [
      { heading: "A, S and I regulator functions", paragraphs: ["A is the regulator's battery/system sense reference and should see battery voltage through the approved harness connection. S receives the stator-phase signal from the dedicated small stator terminal. I is the ignition/indicator input used to excite the regulator when the key is on.", "Connector letters are authoritative; colors are only a cross-check for a known harness. Reproduction and aftermarket pigtails may use different insulation colors, and a plug viewed from the wire side is mirrored from the alternator terminal face."] },
      { heading: "High-current B+ cable and fuse", paragraphs: ["The threaded B+ stud carries charging output separately from the regulator plug. Route it to the battery-positive distribution point through a charge cable and high-current fuse/fusible link rated for the alternator, cable and kit—not merely for the old factory load.", "Mount protection close to the source/distribution point as the kit specifies, shield the cable from headers and abrasion, and support it so vibration does not loosen the alternator stud. Never place the full 3G output through an original dashboard ammeter loop not designed for it."] },
      { heading: "1G external-regulator conversion", paragraphs: ["A 1G vehicle has a fender-mounted external regulator and separate field/stator/indicator wiring. A proper conversion harness or regulator-replacement interface captures the original warning-lamp circuit and adapts the required ignition signal while retiring or isolating old field wiring.", "Do not join terminals at the old regulator by trial. Vehicles with a warning lamp and vehicles with ammeter/voltmeter instrumentation can have different original wire counts and excitation requirements."] },
      { heading: "2G conversion and connector fire risk", paragraphs: ["The 2G high-current output used a push-in connector with parallel output conductors. Upgrade instructions from Powermaster explicitly say not to use the OEM 2G-style battery plug on the 3G conversion; the 3G uses its threaded output stud and a new cable.", "Inspect any heat-damaged 2G harness beyond the connector and repair affected insulation/junctions. Installing a new alternator while leaving an overheated connector energized does not remove the fire hazard."] },
      { heading: "Ground, pulley and belt checks", paragraphs: ["Painted or powder-coated brackets can insulate the alternator. Use clean mounting contact and a dedicated case-to-engine ground sized according to the alternator manufacturer; the battery must also have a strong engine-block ground.", "A high-output 3G loads the belt harder at low speed. Confirm pulley ratio, alignment, adequate wrap and the tension procedure for V-belt or serpentine drive. Persistent squeal or black dust is a mechanical drive problem, not a reason to overtighten until bearings fail."] },
      { heading: "Post-installation verification", bullets: ["Battery is fully charged and passes a load/conductance test before alternator evaluation.", "Key on/engine off: warning/excitation circuit behaves as designed.", "Engine running: alternator B+ and battery voltages are compared.", "Full load: positive drop from B+ to battery and case-to-negative drop are measured.", "Output cable, fuse holder and terminals are checked for abnormal heating.", "Engine stops normally with the key; no alternator feedback keeps ignition powered.", "Original ammeter behavior is documented; add a voltmeter if the bypassed charge path makes it misleading."] },
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: "What do A, S and I mean on a Ford 3G alternator?", a: "A is battery/system sense, S is the stator-signal connection and I is the ignition/indicator excitation input." },
      { q: "Can I reuse the Ford 2G alternator output plug?", a: "No for the documented 3G upgrade. Use the threaded B+ stud and a new protected cable as the conversion manufacturer specifies." },
      { q: "Where should the 3G B+ cable connect?", a: "To the battery-positive/common junction—often the battery side of the fender starter relay—through correctly sized high-current protection." },
      { q: "Will the stock ammeter still work accurately?", a: "Often not after a new output cable bypasses its original shunt/path. Follow the conversion instructions and consider a voltmeter." },
      { q: "Why does my 3G alternator not charge at idle?", a: "Check I excitation, A sense voltage, S/stator connection, belt slip/pulley ratio, B+ protection, grounds and the alternator before replacing parts." },
    ],
