          "A 2V engine is single-overhead-cam with one camshaft per cylinder bank and no 3V-style cam phaser. Its timing set has a crank sprocket, two long primary chains, two cam sprockets, bank-specific guides and hydraulic tensioners. Ford's M-6004-462V instructions cover the replacement architecture but also flag an early-engine guide-dowel modification, showing why even 2V years are not automatically identical.",
          "A 3V engine remains SOHC but uses variable cam timing. Large phaser assemblies replace ordinary fixed cam sprockets, and service may require crank and phaser holding tools plus phaser/oil-control checks. Ford lists its 4.6L 3V camshaft-drive kit for 2005-2010 engines, but the vehicle workshop manual still controls the installation.",
