    title: "Chevy LS3 Timing Chain Alignment: Production Single-Bolt Cam Guide",
    metaDescription: "Chevrolet LS3 timing-chain alignment guide for the production non-VVT single-bolt cam sprocket, dot-to-dot reference, tensioner, oil-pump clearance and verification.",
    answer: "A production Chevrolet LS3 is a Gen IV, non-VVT engine with one camshaft in the block, one crank sprocket, one timing chain, a chain tensioner and a single-bolt cam sprocket. During the production service alignment, the crank-sprocket timing mark is placed at 12 o'clock and the cam-sprocket mark at 6 o'clock so the marks face each other on the centerline. This dot-to-dot position is an assembly reference; it places the engine at the cylinder-6 firing position, not cylinder 1 compression. Confirm the exact sprocket and service procedure before tightening because LS376 performance derivatives can use a different three-bolt cam sprocket.",
    detail: "Chevrolet identifies the production LS3 cam as a single-bolt sprocket design and directs service to 2014 Chevrolet SS LS3 information. Chevrolet's upgrade sheet lists 30 N·m (22 lb-ft) for the timing-chain tensioner bolts, but its 30 N·m cam-sprocket entry belongs to the kit's three-bolt sprocket and must not be used on the stock single-bolt LS3. Obtain the current GM single-bolt replacement and torque-angle procedure for the exact vehicle.",
    scope: "Production-style non-VVT LS3 6.2L engines with the single-bolt cam sprocket. Do not apply this diagram unchanged to L92 VVT, aftermarket adjustable sets, LS376 three-bolt conversions, dry-sump combinations, or another LS/LSX engine without verifying parts and service data.",
