    answer: "On a conventional Dodge/Chrysler 360 timing set, align the crankshaft-sprocket mark at 12 o'clock and the camshaft-sprocket mark at 6 o'clock so both lie on the exact centerline through the two shaft bores. Assemble the chain around both sprockets on the bench, spread the sprockets apart, verify the marks with a straightedge, and slide both sprockets evenly onto the crank and cam together. This is mechanical cam timing, not the vehicle's ignition-timing value.",
    detail: "A 360 can be an LA carbureted engine or a 5.9L Magnum EFI engine, and most production 360 combinations are externally balanced. The offset weight in the damper or converter/flywheel is not a timing mark and must not be substituted or mixed with 318 neutral-balance hardware. Dot-to-dot places cylinder 6 in its firing position; rotate the crank one full revolution before setting the distributor for cylinder 1 compression.",
