    metaDescription: "VW/Audi 1.8T 20V timing-belt guide explaining cam-sprocket and crank/flywheel TDC marks, longitudinal vs transverse layouts, tensioners and the rear cam chain.",
    answer: "On a VW/Audi 1.8T 20V, set cylinder 1 to TDC/compression and align the exhaust-cam sprocket mark with the pointer or edge on the rear timing cover/cylinder-head cover specified for the engine code. Verify the crank independently: transverse AWW/AWP-style procedures may use the flywheel TDC mark through the transmission bellhousing window, while longitudinal AWM/AMB procedures commonly show the crank pulley mark against the lower timing cover, with a flywheel/flexplate reference as an additional check. The belt drives the exhaust cam only; the intake cam is synchronized to it by the short chain and hydraulic tensioner at the rear of the head.",
    detail: "Do not rely on paint marks copied from the old belt. The 1.8T is an interference engine, the four-bolt outer crank pulley can be removed without loosening the center crank-sprocket bolt in many procedures, and early versus late hydraulic belt tensioners use different compression/locking and adjustment tools. Decode the stamped engine code before selecting marks or parts.",
    scope: "Volkswagen/Audi 1.8T 20-valve engine families such as AEB, ATW, AWM, AWW, AWP and AMB only after exact engine-code, vehicle and tensioner identification. It does not cover later chain-driven EA888 1.8 TSI engines, older 8-valve 1.8 engines or a full intake/exhaust cam-chain service.",
