    metaDescription: "Ford 351M head-bolt guide for stock iron heads: 65, 75, then 95–105 lb-ft in sequence, thread preparation, engine ID and aftermarket cautions.",
    answer: "For a stock Ford 351M with original-style reusable 1/2-inch head bolts, cast-iron block and cast-iron heads, period Ford service information gives a final head-bolt specification of 95–105 lb-ft. A period rebuild procedure reaches it in three complete sequence passes: 65 lb-ft, 75 lb-ft, then 95–105 lb-ft. The factory convention calls for clean fasteners with engine oil on the threads unless the application or bolt maker specifically requires sealer. Follow the correct ten-bolt-per-bank center-out Ford figure on every pass.",
    detail: "The 351M belongs to Ford's 335-series family and shares much cylinder-head architecture with the 400 and 351 Cleveland, but it is not a 351 Windsor. Vintage production bolts are conventional torque fasteners, not modern torque-to-yield bolts. Aftermarket aluminum heads, studs, washers, lubricants and composition gaskets can change both torque and retorque instructions, so the component maker's sheet overrides the stock values.",
    scope: "Stock-style 1975–1982 Ford 351M/5.8L engines with production cast-iron heads, an unmodified cast-iron block, correct gasket and serviceable OEM-type head bolts. 351 Windsor, Boss/HO 351 Cleveland, 400-only vehicle details, aluminum heads, studs and repaired or decked combinations require separate instructions.",
