    metaDescription: "Dodge 4.7L head-bolt guide comparing early 15/35 lb-ft +90° and later 20 lb-ft +90° +90° methods, small sealed bolts and reuse inspection.",
    answer: "Dodge/Chrysler 4.7L head-bolt procedures changed. A cited 2005 Ram and 2008 Aspen use ten main bolts at 20 N·m (15 lb-ft), then 47 N·m (35 lb-ft), then +90°; their four smaller bolts are tightened to 25 N·m (18 lb-ft) during stage 2 and 30 N·m (22 lb-ft) after the angle pass. A cited 2009 Grand Cherokee uses 27 N·m (20 lb-ft) on main bolts 1–10, repeats that torque without loosening, tightens small bolts 11–14 to 14 N·m (124 lb-in), then adds +90° and another +90° to bolts 1–10 and finishes 11–14 at 26 N·m (19 lb-ft). Select by VIN-specific service information, not a generic 4.7L label.",
    detail: "The ten main-bolt threads receive clean engine oil; the four smaller M8 bolts require the specified Mopar locking/sealing compound because their holes can leak. Factory procedures allow main-bolt reuse only after a straightedge confirms every thread crest remains in contact—any necked section requires replacement. Unknown-history hardware is safer replaced as a matched set.",
    scope: "Chrysler/Dodge/Jeep 4.7L PowerTech V8 applications only after VIN, model year, vehicle line, engine build and fastener identification. It excludes the related 3.7L V6, 5.7L HEMI and aftermarket heads/studs. The 2008 transition area must be checked directly because vehicle applications do not all follow one calendar-year procedure.",
